WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.911
DAST requires written authorization because it interacts with live application behavior.

2
00:00:05.911 --> 00:00:15.460
Even when the intent is defensive, scanning can create traffic, trigger logs, exercise forms, use accounts, touch data, and interact with controls.

3
00:00:15.460 --> 00:00:26.904
A test plan should define approved targets, environments, timing, accounts, exclusions, safety limits, data handling expectations, escalation contacts, and rollback paths.

4
00:00:26.904 --> 00:00:32.361
Authorization protects the organization, the testers, and the application owners.

5
00:00:32.361 --> 00:00:35.620
Production and staging have different tradeoffs.

6
00:00:35.620 --> 00:00:42.061
Staging may be safer for active testing because it can use test data and controlled reset procedures.

7
00:00:42.061 --> 00:00:50.398
Production may reveal real configuration, routing, authentication, and deployment behavior that staging does not fully match.

8
00:00:50.398 --> 00:01:01.387
If production testing is approved, scope, rate limits, test windows, excluded operations, monitoring coordination, and support contacts become especially important.

9
00:01:01.387 --> 00:01:04.873
DAST planning should include test accounts and roles.

10
00:01:04.873 --> 00:01:08.359
Unauthenticated scanning sees only public behavior.

11
00:01:08.359 --> 00:01:16.847
Authenticated testing can exercise account-specific workflows, role-based features, tenant boundaries, and session behavior.

12
00:01:16.847 --> 00:01:22.531
Those accounts should use test data, limited permissions, and controlled credentials.

13
00:01:22.531 --> 00:01:29.807
The plan should also describe how accounts are created, protected, monitored, reset, and retired after testing.

14
00:01:29.807 --> 00:01:33.596
Coordination matters because DAST touches several teams.

15
00:01:33.596 --> 00:01:36.779
Application owners understand business impact.

16
00:01:36.779 --> 00:01:40.114
Security teams manage testing intent and evidence.

17
00:01:40.114 --> 00:01:43.145
Operations teams watch availability and logs.

18
00:01:43.145 --> 00:01:47.086
Support teams may receive user reports if testing is visible.

19
00:01:47.086 --> 00:01:54.892
Unauthorized scanning is not acceptable because it ignores ownership, safety, legal boundaries, and operational risk.

20
00:01:54.892 --> 00:01:59.591
Responsible DAST begins with clear permission and shared expectations.
